consiSt("ut",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Utah Supreme Court","d":"Court of last resort. Exclusive appellate jurisdiction over capital cases, lawyer discipline, election and redistricting cases, removal of public officers, and (since 2026) judgments of three-judge district court panels; original appellate jurisdiction over most other civil appeals from district court, which it may “pour over” to the Court of Appeals. Reviews the Court of Appeals by discretionary certiorari, answers questions certified by federal courts, and regulates the practice of law."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Utah Court of Appeals","d":"Hears appeals from juvenile courts, domestic relations appeals, most agency reviews, criminal appeals other than first-degree and capital felonies, and cases transferred by the Supreme Court. May certify a case to the Supreme Court by a vote of four judges."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts (with court commissioners)","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized in judicial districts. Court commissioners hear many domestic-relations motions under Civil Rule 101, and their recommendations are orders unless a party objects within 14 days. Since 2026 a party in a constitutional challenge against the State seeking declaratory or injunctive relief may require a randomly drawn three-judge district court panel (Utah Code § 78A-5-102.7)."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Business and Chancery Court","d":"Court with jurisdiction concurrent with the district court over business, contract, fiduciary, trade-secret, securities, and similar actions seeking at least $300,000 or solely equitable relief (its procedure rules took effect September 1, 2024). It cannot hear actions in which a governmental entity is a party, and generally may not take supplemental consumer-contract, personal-injury, or domestic claims. The judge is the trier of fact unless a party with a jury right timely demands a jury, in which case the claim is transferred to district court. Uses the Rules of Business and Chancery Procedure on top of the Civil Rules."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Juvenile Courts and Justice Courts","d":"Juvenile courts hear child welfare and delinquency matters (appeals go to the Court of Appeals). Justice courts are local courts of limited jurisdiction; their structure was not part of the collected texts."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (2023)","d":"Utah adopted the Uniform Public Expression Protection Act (UPEPA), effective May 3, 2023. It applies to civil actions filed, and causes of action asserted, on or after May 3, 2023; earlier claims are saved. The former narrow anti-SLAPP part of the Judicial Code (78B-6-1401 et seq.) shows no current version on the Legislature’s site, consistent with repeal, but that was not separately confirmed.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-101, -113, -114 (Laws 2023, ch. 488; § 114 amended Laws 2024, ch. 381)","v":true},{"t":"Scope: three kinds of protected activity","d":"Covers any cause of action (including counterclaims, crossclaims, and third-party claims) based on: (a) communication in a legislative, executive, judicial, administrative, or other governmental proceeding; (b) communication on an issue under consideration in such a proceeding; or (c) exercise of freedom of speech or press, assembly, petition, or association under the U.S. or Utah Constitution on a matter of public concern.\nExclusions: claims against governmental units or employees acting officially; government enforcement actions to protect against an imminent threat to public health or safety; and claims against sellers or lessors of goods or services arising from communications about their own goods or services (expressive works are not “goods or services”). The Act must be broadly construed to protect these rights.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-102, -111","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: 60 days to file, automatic stay, 60-day hearing and ruling","d":"File a special motion for expedited relief within 60 days after service of the pleading asserting the claim, or later for good cause. Filing automatically stays all other proceedings between the movant and the responding party, including discovery and pending motions, until the ruling and the time to appeal pass; the court may stay related proceedings with other parties. Limited discovery is allowed only if specific information is needed to meet a burden and is not otherwise reasonably available.\nThe court must hear the motion within 60 days after filing (later for allowed discovery or good cause) and rule within 60 days after the hearing. Fee motions, voluntary dismissals, severance, unrelated motions for good cause, and public-safety injunctions are not stayed.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-103, -104, -105, -108","v":true},{"t":"Standard: dismissal with prejudice","d":"The court considers the pleadings, motion papers, and evidence admissible on summary judgment. It must dismiss with prejudice if the movant shows the Act applies, the responding party fails to show an exclusion, and either the responding party fails to establish a prima facie case on each essential element or the movant shows failure to state a claim or no genuine issue of material fact. A voluntary dismissal without prejudice does not defeat the motion or fees; a dismissal with prejudice makes the movant the prevailing party.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-106, -107","v":true},{"t":"Fees: mandatory for a prevailing movant","d":"The court must award court costs, reasonable attorney fees, and reasonable litigation expenses related to the motion to a prevailing movant, and to a prevailing responding party only if the motion was frivolous or filed solely to delay.","c":"Utah Code § 78B-25-110","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: as of right, 21 days, with a full stay","d":"A moving party may appeal as of right from an order denying the motion in whole or in part. The notice of appeal is due 21 days after entry of the order (not the usual 30). While the appeal is pending, all proceedings between all parties in the action are stayed until it concludes, subject to the statutory exceptions.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-104(3), -109; Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: Bar application first, then a motion in each case","d":"A lawyer not admitted in Utah must be admitted pro hac vice before appearing in any Utah court or state or local administrative body, and for court-annexed, court-ordered, or statutorily mandated arbitration or ADR, including discovery and settlement work incident to those proceedings. Private, voluntary arbitration is not covered.\nEligibility: not a Utah Bar member, not a Utah resident, not regularly employed in Utah, active and in good standing elsewhere, and associated with active Utah local counsel. A Utah resident may be admitted only after receiving a Practice Pending Admission Certificate. Admission is discretionary and may be denied or revoked if used to circumvent normal admission; the tribunal may weigh familiarity with Utah rules, availability, discipline in the prior 5 years, and conduct.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(a)-(f)","v":true},{"t":"What the application needs and what it costs","d":"Submit a sworn application to the Utah State Bar for each matter, listing the tribunal and case, the client, other Utah pro hac vice matters in the past 5 years, any current suspension, discipline in the past 5 years, or pending proceedings, consent to Utah Office of Professional Conduct jurisdiction, and local counsel’s details. Attach a certificate of good standing dated no more than 60 days before the application.\nFee: equal to the current dues of an active Utah Bar member for that licensing year, paid to the Bar (the dollar amount is not in the rule; check with the Bar). Waived for qualifying pro bono matters and for lawyers representing the United States.\nThe Bar issues an Acknowledgment; local counsel then files it with a motion, the application, the good-standing certificate, a proposed order, and proof of service.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(g)-(j)","v":true},{"t":"Local counsel’s role and the bar on early appearances","d":"Local counsel’s name, bar number, and address must appear on every filing; local counsel must personally appear at conferences and hearings when the tribunal deems it appropriate, shares joint responsibility to the client, the court, and opponents, and stays of record until another Utah lawyer is substituted.\nThe applicant may not appear or have its name on any pleading until the order granting admission is entered.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(k)-(l)","v":true},{"t":"Annual renewal and appeals","d":"On or before each anniversary of the Bar application, the lawyer must certify the case is still pending (or concluded) and, within 28 days of the anniversary, pay an annual fee equal to active Bar dues. Failure suspends the lawyer from appearing after 28 days; reinstatement requires the fees plus a $50 late penalty. On appeal, the admitted lawyer files only a notice of appearance in the appellate court. Pro hac vice lawyers must report disciplinary developments to the Bar.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(m)-(p)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice and remote work under Rule 5.5","d":"A lawyer admitted elsewhere may provide temporary services in Utah in association with an actively participating Utah lawyer, or related to pending or expected proceedings or ADR, or arising out of the lawyer’s home practice. Utah also expressly lets a lawyer physically located in Utah serve clients of the jurisdiction where the lawyer is admitted, remotely, as long as the lawyer opens no public-facing Utah office and does not hold out as admitted in Utah.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 5.5(b)-(c) & cmt. [4a]","v":true},{"t":"Moving to Utah: admission by motion, UBE transfer, practice pending admission","d":"Admission by motion requires admission by exam elsewhere, an approved law-school degree, the MPRE, a reciprocal jurisdiction, and full-time practice for 36 of the past 60 months in that jurisdiction (proved by clear and convincing evidence). A transferred UBE score must be at least 270 (pre-July 2023 Legacy UBE) or 260 (July 2023 or later) and is transferable for 36 months; processing takes at least 4 months.\nA lawyer with a pending application may practice from a Utah office for up to 18 months under a Practice Pending Admission Certificate, supervised by a Utah lawyer in the same Utah firm, with a required disclosure statement on all communications.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-705, 14-712, 14-809","v":true},{"t":"Pro bono authorization for out-of-state lawyers","d":"A lawyer in good standing elsewhere may provide uncompensated pro bono services in Utah through an approved sponsoring organization after filing a statement with the Bar, renewed annually; the lawyer must tell the court at the first appearance that the lawyer appears under this rule. It does not authorize other practice without pro hac vice admission.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-803","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances","d":"An attorney may appear for limited purposes (a filing, a motion, a discovery event, a hearing) by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance signed by attorney and client; documents within that scope are served on both. Opposing lawyers may contact a limited-scope client directly about matters outside the scope unless told otherwise, because the limited-scope lawyer must disclose the limits. The limited-scope lawyer withdraws by notice when the task is done.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 5(b)(1)(A), 74(c), 75; Utah R. Prof. Conduct 4.2(b) & cmt. [7], 4.3(b)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access","d":"In district court civil and probate cases, pleadings must be e-filed by an attorney (or licensed paralegal practitioner) of record using the electronic filer’s interface and a unique identifier from the filer’s service provider; hardship exemptions require Judicial Council approval. E-filing serves anyone with an e-filing account. Service on an out-of-state attorney without an e-filing account may be made by email to the address on file with the attorney’s licensing entity. In the appellate courts, all licensed attorneys must e-file. In practice, plan for local counsel’s account to handle trial-court filings.","c":"Utah Code Jud. Admin. R. 4-503; Utah R. Civ. P. 5(b)(3), (e); Utah R. App. P. 21(a)","v":true},{"t":"Licensed paralegal practitioners on the other side","d":"Utah licenses paralegal practitioners (LPPs) to practice in limited areas defined in Rule 14-802; the Civil Rules treat them as attorneys within that scope, and the Rules of Professional Conduct apply to them. Expect LPPs in family, eviction, and debt-collection matters.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 86; Utah R. Prof. Conduct Preliminary Note","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Utah Rules of Professional Conduct (Chapter 13)","d":"Utah’s Rules of Professional Conduct follow the ABA Model Rules with Utah changes, each flagged in a comment saying the rule “differs from the ABA Model Rule.” They also govern licensed paralegal practitioners, so “lawyer” includes LPPs unless a rule says otherwise. Comments explain and do not add duties. Several rules (1.0, 1.4, 1.5, 5.4, 5.8, 8.4) were amended effective November 1, 2026.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct Preliminary Note; Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. ch. 13","v":true},{"t":"Referral fees banned; fee sharing between lawyers under new Rule 5.8","d":"As of November 1, 2026, referral fees (compensation paid solely for referring a matter to someone outside the firm) are prohibited; lead-generation and advertising payments are not referral fees. Fee sharing with lawyers in other firms is allowed only if no one is paid before the fee is payable, the total fee does not increase, and the client agrees in a confirmed writing, including each lawyer’s share, and each share must be reasonable. The text does not repeat the ABA requirement that the division be proportional to work or that each lawyer assume joint responsibility.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 1.0(d), (q) & cmt. [9], 1.5(e), 5.4(c)-(d), 5.8","v":true},{"t":"Nonlawyer ownership and fee sharing through the Supreme Court’s authorization","d":"Unlike ABA Model Rule 5.4, a Utah lawyer may share fees with nonlawyers, or practice in an entity with nonlawyer owners or managers, if the arrangement or entity has been authorized under Utah Supreme Court Standing Order No. 15 and the lawyer gives clients the required written notice (before taking the representation) of the fee-sharing or ownership structure. Standing Order No. 15 itself was not collected.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 5.4(e)-(f) & cmt. [6]","v":true},{"t":"Candor: recklessness is enough","d":"Utah’s Rule 3.3 was amended after In re Larsen, 2016 UT 26, so that a lawyer violates the rule by recklessly (not only knowingly) making a false statement of law or fact to a tribunal or failing to disclose controlling adverse authority.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 3.3 cmt. [3]","v":true},{"t":"Conflicts and files: lateral screening, indigent expenses, client files","d":"Utah permits screening to remove imputation when a lateral lawyer is disqualified under Rule 1.9, without the client’s consent, though courts may demand more on disqualification motions. Rule 1.8(e) lets lawyers pay minor litigation-related expenses (transportation, lodging) for indigent clients. On termination, the lawyer must give the client the file on request notwithstanding attorney lien laws or unpaid fees; work product such as research notes and internal memoranda generally is not part of the file.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 1.10 cmt. [5a], 1.8 cmt. [10a], 1.16 cmt. [9]","v":true},{"t":"Represented persons and limited-scope clients","d":"Rule 4.2 adds paragraphs (b)-(e): a lawyer may deal directly with a person represented only on a limited basis about matters outside that scope (the limited-scope lawyer must disclose the limits), and government lawyers in criminal and civil enforcement have specific authority to contact represented persons. Rule 4.2(a) also recognizes “judicially created rules” as authority for contact.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 4.2 & cmts. [1], [7], 4.3(b) & cmt. [3a]","v":true},{"t":"Misconduct: no ABA 8.4(g), but civility counts","d":"Utah’s Rule 8.4 has no counterpart to ABA Model Rule 8.4(g) on harassment and discrimination; bias manifested in representation is addressed under 8.4(d) when prejudicial to the administration of justice. An egregious violation or a pattern of violations of the Standards of Professionalism and Civility may support an 8.4(d) violation, and conditioning a settlement on not filing a bar complaint is prejudicial to the administration of justice. Rule 8.4(c) allows lawful investigative deception (stings, fair-housing testers).","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 8.4 & cmts. [2a], [3], [3a], [3b], [6]","v":true},{"t":"Standards of Professionalism and Civility","d":"The Supreme Court’s civility standards (Rule 14-301) apply in all Utah proceedings and set expectations for courtesy, no unfounded attacks on opposing counsel’s motives, and decorum in depositions, email, and other exchanges. Lawyers must tell clients that civility is expected; Practice Pending Admission supervisors must ensure compliance.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-301; 14-809(a)(1)(B)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising and solicitation: Utah’s single Rule 7.1","d":"Utah folds its advertising rules into one Rule 7.1: no false or misleading communications (including unjustified-expectation and testimonial rules), no coercive or harassing contacts, and no direct communication with a prospective client about a disaster, personal injury, or wrongful death within 30 days of the event unless there is a family, close personal, or prior professional relationship or the prospect initiated it. Utah has not adopted Model Rule 7.6.","c":"Utah R. Prof. Conduct 7.1 & cmt. [12], 7.6","v":true}],"style":"State of Utah","home":"https://www.utcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Your tier pleading caps your damages","d":"A damages claimant must plead an amount or a discovery tier, and the tier must appear in the caption. Pleading Tier 1 or Tier 2 waives damages above that tier’s limit ($50,000, or under $300,000) unless the pleading is amended under Rule 15.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 8(a), 10(a)(1), 26(c)(3)"},{"t":"No discovery until you serve your own initial disclosures, and the clock is already running","d":"A party may not seek discovery from any source until its own Rule 26(a)(1) disclosures are served (plaintiff: 14 days after the first answer; defendant: 42 days after its answer). Standard fact discovery runs only 120, 180, or 210 days by tier from the first defendant’s first disclosure deadline, and a statement of discovery issues does not toll it. Undisclosed witnesses and documents are excluded unless harmless or for good cause.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 26(a)(2), (c)(2), (c)(5), (d)(4), 37(a)(9)"},{"t":"Expert discovery is lost if you do not elect within 14 days","d":"After an expert disclosure, the opposing party has 14 days to elect a 4-hour deposition or a written report; with no election, no further discovery of that expert is allowed. An expert who gives a report cannot be deposed.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 26(a)(4)(B)-(C), 30(a)"},{"t":"Fully briefed motions sit until someone files a Request to Submit","d":"A motion is not submitted for decision until a party files a “Request to Submit for Decision” after briefing closes. Oppositions are due 14 days after the motion and replies 7 days after the opposition. Evidence objections go in the next memorandum, not a separate motion to strike.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 7(d)-(e), (g), (n)"},{"t":"Seven days to use a peremptory change of judge","d":"Where Rule 63A applies (counties with seven or more district judges), the defendant side’s notice is due within 7 days after service or at its first filing, whichever is first; missing it forfeits the right. Confirm the rule’s effective status first.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 63A(a)(2)"},{"t":"Out-of-state counsel cannot sign anything until the order is entered","d":"A pro hac vice applicant may not appear or have its name on any pleading until the court grants the motion, and the motion can be filed only after the Bar issues its Acknowledgment. Build in time for the Bar step, the 60-day good-standing certificate, and annual renewal within 28 days of each anniversary.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(g), (i)-(l), (n)-(o)"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP: 60 days to move, 21 days to appeal","d":"The UPEPA special motion is due 60 days after service of the pleading, and an order denying it must be appealed within 21 days, not 30. The motion triggers an automatic stay of discovery between the parties and mandatory fees for a prevailing movant.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-103, -104, -110; Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2)"},{"t":"Rule 60(b) motions for mistake or fraud: 90 days, not one year","d":"Relief for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be sought within 90 days after entry of the judgment or order. Separately, a Rule 60(b) motion tolls the appeal time only if filed within 28 days after judgment.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 60(c); Utah R. App. P. 4(b)(1)(E)"},{"t":"Summons, service, and commencement work differently","d":"The plaintiff’s attorney signs and issues the summons. An action can start by serving the summons and complaint before filing, but then the complaint, summons, and proof of service must be filed within 10 days or the action is deemed dismissed. Serve within 120 days after filing. Answers are due in 21 days, or 30 days if served outside Utah.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 3(a), 4(a)-(b), 12(a)"},{"t":"Different mail-service add-ons at trial and on appeal","d":"In the trial court, 7 days are added only when service is exclusively by mail; on appeal, 3 days are added for mail service, and appellate periods under 11 days exclude weekends and holidays.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 6(c); Utah R. App. P. 22(a), (d)"},{"t":"Jury waived if the jury fee is not paid","d":"A jury demand must be served within 14 days after the last pleading directed to the issue and the statutory jury fee paid; failing to pay the fee or to serve and file the demand waives the jury.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 38(b), (d)"},{"t":"Appeal extras: $300 bond, docketing statement, paper copies","d":"File a $300 cost bond with the notice of appeal (unless an exception applies), a docketing statement within 21 days, and transcript orders within 21 days after that. Even after e-filing a brief, deliver 10 (Supreme Court) or 6 (Court of Appeals) paper copies, binder-clipped with the right cover color, within 7 days.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 6, 9(b), 11(c), 26(d), 27(e)"}],"judge":[{"t":"New: peremptory change of judge in larger counties (Rule 63A)","d":"Effective February 13, 2026, in a civil action in a county with seven or more district court judges, each side gets one change of judge as of right by filing a notice with the clerk, without stating reasons but attesting it is not for delay or based on race, gender, or religious affiliation. Deadlines are short: plaintiff side within 7 days after a judge is first assigned; defendant side within 7 days after service or at its first filing, whichever comes first; panel cases within 7 days after notice of the assigned judges. Excluded: Rule 65C post-conviction cases, juvenile court, the Business and Chancery Court, water and tax judges, remands, and most modification petitions before the same judge. Which counties currently qualify is not stated in the rule.\nThe rule text says its February 13, 2026 effective date depends on a constitutional two-thirds legislative vote; confirm it is in force before relying on it.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 63A(a)","v":true},{"t":"Change of judge by unanimous agreement","d":"All parties may jointly change the judge once, without cause, by a notice signed by all parties filed within 90 days after commencement or before the trial-setting notice, whichever comes first. No one may tell the court who sought consent.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 63A(b)","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification for cause: 21 days, one motion","d":"A motion to disqualify for bias, prejudice, or conflict needs a good-faith certificate and an affidavit or unsworn declaration of facts, and must be filed within 21 days after the latest of assignment, appearance, or when the grounds were or should have been known. The challenged judge must grant it or certify it to a reviewing judge, without a response, and take no further action meanwhile. Only one motion per party absent new grounds; signing it carries Rule 11 exposure.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 63(b)-(c)","v":true},{"t":"Three-judge district court panels","d":"In a civil action against the State or a state official challenging the constitutionality of a statute, constitutional provision, legislative action, executive order, or rule and seeking declaratory or injunctive relief, a party may file a notice within 45 days after commencement (or an amended complaint) to require a panel of three randomly selected judges from different judicial districts. The assigned judge must transfer the case immediately; venue statutes do not apply; dispositive motions, injunctions, and trial are heard en banc; appeals go directly to the Supreme Court. Each side may also change one panel judge as of right, and individual panel judges (not the panel) can be disqualified.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78A-5-102.7, 78A-3-102(3)(a)(x); Utah R. Civ. P. 42(f), 63(d), 63A(a)(1)(B)","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"UPEPA: broad construction for speech, press, petition, and association rights","d":"Utah’s anti-SLAPP statute expressly protects rights “guaranteed by the United States Constitution or Utah Constitution” and directs courts to construe it broadly to protect those rights, with uniformity among enacting states considered.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-102(2)(c), -111, -112","v":true},{"t":"Utah Constitution free-speech provisions not collected","d":"Whether the Utah Constitution’s speech and press guarantees are read more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked; the constitutional text and case law were not collected.","c":"Utah Const. art. I (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Tiered, proportional discovery with the burden on the requester","d":"Discovery limits depend on the damages pleaded: Tier 1 ($50,000 or less), Tier 2 (more than $50,000 and under $300,000, or non-monetary relief), Tier 3 ($300,000 or more), Tier 4 (domestic). The party seeking discovery always bears the burden of showing proportionality, and going past standard limits requires a stipulation (with a discovery budget since November 1, 2026) or a statement of discovery issues.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 26(b)(3)-(4), (c)(3)-(6), 29, 37(a)","v":true},{"t":"Business and Chancery Court","d":"A plaintiff that files there waives a jury on its own claims; a defendant must demand a jury with its first responsive pleading or waive it, and a jury demand with a right to jury sends the claim to district court. Presumptive discovery there is 20 interrogatories, 25 document requests, 30 admission requests, and 10 depositions per side, completed within 210 days. A party may also ask the district court to transfer a qualifying case there, mandatory if requested within 21 days of appearance.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78A-5a-103, -104; Utah R. Bus. & Chancery P. 26(c)(3), 38; Utah R. Civ. P. 42(e)(2)","v":true},{"t":"Non-unanimous civil juries","d":"A civil verdict needs agreement of three-fourths of the jurors unless the parties stipulate otherwise, and parties may stipulate to fewer than eight jurors.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 47(r), 48","v":true},{"t":"Two-way offers of settlement","d":"Either side may make a Rule 68 offer (more than 14 days before trial, open at least 14 days). An offeree who does not beat it loses post-offer costs, prejudgment interest, and attorney fees and pays the offeror’s post-offer costs.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 68","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Utah Rules of Appellate Procedure (all active rules as displayed on legacy.utcourts.gov, printed November 10, 2026, including amendments effective November 1, 2026); Utah Rules of Civil Procedure 50, 52, 54, 58A, 59, 60, 62, 73; Utah Code §§ 78A-3-102 (eff. May 6, 2026), 78A-4-103 (eff. March 13, 2026), 78A-5-102.7 (eff. March 13, 2026), and 78B-25-104, -109 (Uniform Public Expression Protection Act), from le.utah.gov. Summaries are Consi’s.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Utah: one notice, two courts, and a Supreme Court that pours cases over","d":"Utah has two appellate courts. A notice of appeal is filed in the trial court within 30 days after entry of the judgment, and the appeal is docketed in whichever appellate court has statutory jurisdiction. The Supreme Court has original appellate jurisdiction over most civil appeals from district court but may transfer (“pour over”) them to the Court of Appeals; the Court of Appeals hears domestic relations appeals, juvenile appeals, most agency reviews, and non-first-degree criminal appeals directly. The Supreme Court reviews the Court of Appeals only by discretionary writ of certiorari.\nThe 30-day notice deadline, the interlocutory-petition deadline, and the certiorari deadline are jurisdictional: the appellate court cannot suspend them (Rule 2). Watch the short deadlines that catch newcomers: 10 days in eviction cases, 21 days to appeal a denial of an anti-SLAPP motion, 21 days to petition for interlocutory review, and a $300 cost bond with the notice.\nLicensed attorneys must e-file in the appellate courts, but paper copies of briefs are still required.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 21, 26, 42, 48; Utah Code §§ 78A-3-102, 78A-4-103"},"groups":[["Which court",[{"t":"Supreme Court: exclusive jurisdiction it cannot transfer","d":"The Supreme Court alone hears, and may not transfer: Court of Appeals judgments (by certiorari) and cases certified by the Court of Appeals; lawyer discipline; Judicial Conduct Commission orders; capital cases; legislative-subpoena rulings; election, voting-contest, and redistricting judgments and interlocutory appeals; retention or removal of public officers; and, new in 2026, judgments and interlocutory appeals from a three-judge district court panel under § 78A-5-102.7.","c":"Utah Code § 78A-3-102(3)"},{"t":"Supreme Court: transferable (“pour-over”) jurisdiction","d":"The Supreme Court also has original appellate jurisdiction over first-degree felony appeals, final orders in formal adjudications of listed agencies (Public Service Commission, Tax Commission, and others), facial-unconstitutionality judgments, and any court-of-record judgment the Court of Appeals cannot hear, which in practice includes ordinary civil appeals from district court. It may transfer any of these to the Court of Appeals.\nBefore transferring, the Court issues a notice of intent to transfer; a party may ask it to keep the case by letter within 10 days (issues and reasons limited to 5 pages, with a jurisdiction checklist), and any response is due within 5 days.","c":"Utah Code § 78A-3-102(4); Utah R. App. P. 42"},{"t":"Court of Appeals: direct jurisdiction","d":"Appeals from juvenile courts; domestic relations appeals (divorce, custody, support, parent-time, adoption, paternity); most agency adjudications and district-court review of local-agency decisions; criminal appeals and interlocutory appeals other than first-degree and capital felonies; certain extraordinary-writ appeals by prisoners and Board of Pardons challenges; Utah Military Court appeals; and cases poured over by the Supreme Court. With the votes of four judges it may certify a case to the Supreme Court; a party may suggest certification (5 pages) after the docketing statement is filed.","c":"Utah Code § 78A-4-103(3), (5); Utah R. App. P. 43"},{"t":"Filed in the wrong appellate court","d":"A timely notice, interlocutory petition, or petition for review filed in the wrong appellate court is transferred to the right one, and deadlines then follow the receiving court’s schedule. A notice of appeal sent to an appellate court instead of the trial court is forwarded and treated as filed on the date the appellate court received it.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 44"}]],["Starting the appeal",[{"t":"Notice of appeal: 30 days from entry, filed in the trial court","d":"File the notice with the trial court clerk within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order (10 days in forcible entry and unlawful detainer cases; 21 days from an order denying, in whole or part, an anti-SLAPP motion under § 78B-25-103). If the judgment is entered on a weekend or holiday, entry is deemed the next business day. The notice must name the appealing parties, the courts, and the judgment, and say whether privately retained trial counsel will stay on. Serve it on all parties. A notice filed after a decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 3(a), (d)-(e), 4(a), (c)"},{"t":"When is a judgment “entered”?","d":"A judgment must be in a separate document, and it is entered when the judge signs it and it is recorded in the docket, or 150 days after the decision is docketed if no separate document is entered. A pending fee motion does not affect finality, but the appeal clock runs from its disposition (see tolling).","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 58A(a), (e), (f)"},{"t":"Filing fee and $300 cost bond","d":"Pay the appellate filing fee to the trial court clerk with the notice (the clerk must accept an unpaid notice, but an unpaid fee can lead to dismissal). File a cost bond of at least $300 with the notice unless the adverse party waives it in writing, fees are waived, the appeal is criminal, or the appellant is a government entity or officer; a supersedeas bond replaces it.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 3(f), 6, 21(b)"},{"t":"Docketing statement within 21 days","d":"Within 21 days after the notice of appeal (or cross-appeal or petition for review), file and serve a docketing statement showing jurisdiction (key dates, any tolling motions, any Rule 54(b) certification and what remains below) and at least one substantial issue, without argument. Failure can lead to dismissal of a civil appeal. None is filed after an interlocutory petition is granted unless ordered.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 9"},{"t":"Transcripts and the record","d":"Within 21 days after filing the docketing statement, order transcripts online at utcourts.gov and serve a designation, or file a certificate that none are needed. The appellee may cross-designate within 14 days. If you challenge a finding as unsupported, you must include all evidence relevant to it. Transcripts are due 30 days after payment arrangements; the trial court sends the Bates-paginated record within 20 days of the appellate court’s request.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 11(c), 12"},{"t":"Stays pending appeal","d":"No execution for 28 days after judgment. A supersedeas bond in the presumptive amount (compensatory damages plus costs, fees, and 3 years of interest; capped at $25 million in class or aggregate cases; none for punitive damages) stays a money judgment. Ask the trial court first; an appellate motion must explain why relief was denied below or what extraordinary circumstances excuse asking. The appellate court ordinarily requires security, but may waive it under listed standards.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 62(a), (b), (h); Utah R. App. P. 8"}]],["Tolling and extensions",[{"t":"Motions that restart the clock","d":"A timely motion under Civil Rule 50(b), 52(b), or 59 (new trial or alter or amend), a Rule 60(b) motion filed within 28 days after judgment, or a Rule 73 attorney-fee motion or claim restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. These post-judgment motions are due within 28 days of judgment (fees within 14 days) and the 28-day limits cannot be extended. A notice filed while such a motion is pending covers only the underlying judgment; to appeal the ruling on the motion, file a new or amended notice. Election and redistricting cases are not tolled.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(b); Utah R. Civ. P. 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 73(a)"},{"t":"Extending the notice deadline","d":"The trial court may extend the time on a motion filed before the deadline for good cause, or within 30 days after it for good cause or excusable neglect. No extension may exceed 30 days past the original deadline or 14 days after the order granting it, whichever is later.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(e)"},{"t":"Reinstating the time when notice of judgment never arrived","d":"In a civil case, the trial court must reinstate the 30-day period if the party lacked actual notice of the judgment in time to seek an extension, monitored the case diligently, and the party responsible for serving the signed judgment under Civil Rule 58A did not promptly serve it. Move within one year after entry of judgment; the notice is then due 30 days after the reinstatement order.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(g)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals","d":"Any other party may file a notice of appeal within 14 days after the first notice is filed, or within the regular time, whichever ends later. No cross-appeal may be taken in a granted interlocutory appeal, and cross-petitions for permission to appeal are not allowed.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(d), 5(g), (i)"},{"t":"Computing time","d":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day, rolling past weekends and Utah legal holidays. When a period is shorter than 11 days, intermediate Saturdays, Sundays, and legal holidays are excluded (unlike the Civil Rules). Add 3 days when a document is served by mail (the Civil Rules add 7). Jurisdictional deadlines listed in Rule 2 cannot be extended.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 2, 22(a), (d); compare Utah R. Civ. P. 6(c)"}]],["Briefing",[{"t":"Briefing schedule","d":"Appellant’s principal brief is due 40 days after the clerk’s Rule 13 notice (sent when the record index is filed); appellee’s brief 30 days after service of appellant’s; reply 30 days after appellee’s (at least 5 days before argument if argument is set within 35 days). With a cross-appeal, the cross-appellant’s combined brief and each later brief follow in 30-day steps. If a summary-disposition or Rule 23B motion is filed after the briefing notice, the opening brief is due 30 days after its denial.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 13, 26(a)-(b)"},{"t":"Extensions by stipulation, then by motion","d":"Parties may extend any brief deadline by filing a notice of stipulation before the deadline, effective without an order, up to a total of 60 days per brief. Further extensions require a motion before the deadline showing specific good cause (other litigation identified by caption, complexity, or detailed hardship) and are disfavored.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 22(b)-(c)"},{"t":"Length and content","d":"Principal briefs: 14,000 words or 30 pages; reply briefs 7,000 words or 15 pages (death-penalty cases 28,000/14,000). In cross-appeals the cross-appellant’s combined brief may run 21,000 words or 45 pages. Each issue needs the standard of review and a record cite showing preservation; include an introduction, statement of the case with record cites, explicit attorney-fee requests, a certificate of compliance, and an addendum with the order under review. Cite Utah decisions issued since January 1, 1999 by universal citation (e.g., 2015 UT 99, ¶ 3).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 24(a), (f), (g), 24A(g)"},{"t":"Format, covers, and paper copies","d":"Text and footnotes in 13-point or larger type, double-spaced, 1-inch margins. Brief covers are color-coded (appellant blue, appellee red, intervenor or amicus green, reply gray). Besides e-filing, deliver 10 paper copies to the Supreme Court or 6 to the Court of Appeals within 7 days, printed double-sided on 20 lb. paper and held with a binder clip, not stapled.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 26(d), 27(a), (e)"},{"t":"Constitutional challenges: serve the Attorney General","d":"When a party challenges the constitutionality of a statute and the Attorney General has not appeared, every party must serve its briefs on the Attorney General (notices@agutah.gov) on or before filing and file proof of service; similar service goes to a governmental entity whose ordinance or rule is challenged. The Attorney General then says within 14 days whether it will file an amicus brief.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 25A"},{"t":"Amicus briefs","d":"Only a guardian ad litem, the State, another state’s Attorney General, or the United States may file without consent or leave. Others need all parties’ consent or leave on motion, and must give the parties at least 7 days’ notice of intent to file.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 25(a)-(c)"},{"t":"Motions practice","d":"Responses to motions are due within 14 days after service and replies within 5 days after the response; procedural motions may be decided at once. A motion for expedited review is limited to 5 pages, with any response due within 3 days.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 23, 23C"}]],["Decision, rehearing, and Supreme Court review",[{"t":"Summary disposition and simplified appeals","d":"A party may move at any time to dismiss for lack of jurisdiction (response within 14 days). After the docketing statement, the court may summarily affirm when no substantial question is presented or reverse for manifest error; other deadlines are suspended while such a motion is pending. In the Court of Appeals, a party may ask within 10 days after the docketing statement (or transfer) for the simplified process for appeals applying settled law, with memoranda limited to 7,000 words (reply 3,500).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 10"},{"t":"Oral argument and mediation","d":"The Supreme Court holds argument unless it would not aid decision; the Court of Appeals allows argument only when it would significantly aid decision. The clerk gives at least 28 days’ notice; Court of Appeals parties may waive within 14 days of the notice. The court may order appellate mediation, which stays all appellate deadlines while pending.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 28A(d), 29"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days, response only on request","d":"A petition for rehearing (15 pages, certifying good faith) may be filed within 14 days after an opinion or merits order, stating the points of law or fact overlooked or misapprehended. No response is allowed unless the court requests one, and the court will not grant rehearing without requesting one. Consecutive or late petitions are rejected; clerical errors are raised by letter.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 35"},{"t":"Certiorari to the Supreme Court","d":"Review of a Court of Appeals decision is discretionary, granted mainly for questions with significant precedential value. File the petition within 30 days after the Court of Appeals’ decision (not the remittitur), or after denial of a timely rehearing petition; the limit is 4,000 words or 15 pages. As amended November 1, 2026, no response may be filed unless the Court requests one (then due in 30 days; 4,000 words), and a reply is due 7 days after the response (1,500 words). Cross-petitions are due within the same time or 30 days after the petition. One extension of up to 30 days is available.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 46, 48, 49(d), 50"},{"t":"Remittitur and costs","d":"The Supreme Court issues its remittitur 15 days after judgment (5 days after a rehearing ruling); the Court of Appeals issues it when the certiorari period expires, or 5 days after certiorari is denied. Costs (record, transcripts, printing, bond premiums, filing fees) go to the prevailing party in civil appeals; file a verified bill of costs in the trial court within 14 days after the remittitur is filed there, and objections are due in 7 days. Frivolous or delaying appeals can draw single or double costs and attorney fees, against the party or counsel.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 33, 34, 36"},{"t":"Citing unpublished decisions","d":"Published opinions of either court, and Court of Appeals unpublished decisions issued from October 1, 1998 through December 31, 2010, are precedent. Other unpublished decisions may be cited only if accurate copies are given to the court and all parties when first cited. Decisions by order are not precedent.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 30(e), (g)"}]],["Interlocutory and extraordinary review",[{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal: 21 days","d":"File a petition with the appellate court within 21 days after entry of the trial court’s signed order (since May 1, 2026 the order must be signed, and if a party was told to prepare an order, the clock starts only when that signed order is entered). Limit 20 pages; show preservation, the standard of review, why immediate review is warranted, and how it may materially advance termination. No response unless the court requests one (then due in 14 days) and no reply unless requested. Granted only if the order involves substantial rights and may materially affect the final decision, or early review better serves justice. A non-final Rule 54(b) appeal may be treated as a petition.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 5"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP denials are appealable as of right","d":"An order denying an anti-SLAPP special motion in whole or in part may be appealed as of right by the moving party, within 21 days after entry. A stay of proceedings between the parties lasts until the order and the time to appeal pass, and during an appeal all proceedings between all parties are stayed until the appeal concludes, except fee motions and listed matters.","c":"Utah Code §§ 78B-25-104, -109; Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2)"},{"t":"Extraordinary relief in the appellate courts","d":"When no plain, speedy, and adequate remedy exists (including interlocutory review), a petition for extraordinary relief may be filed in an appellate court, explaining why it is not filed in the trial court. Limit 20 pages or 7,000 words (reply 10 pages or 3,500 words). No response unless requested (then 30 days); a reply is due in 14 days. A single justice or judge may deny a facially frivolous petition, reviewable on request within 7 days.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 19"},{"t":"Certified questions from federal courts","d":"The Utah Supreme Court may answer questions of Utah law certified by any federal court when the question is controlling and no controlling Utah law appears.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 41; Utah Code § 78A-3-102(1)"}]],["Filing mechanics",[{"t":"Appellate e-filing is mandatory for attorneys","d":"All licensed attorneys must file through the appellate e-filing system; unrepresented parties may file by email (supremecourt@utcourts.gov or courtofappeals@utcourts.gov), mail, or in person. Files must be searchable PDFs of no more than 7 MB each. E-filed documents are timely if received by 11:59 p.m. on the due date; briefs are also timely if postmarked by the due date. E-filers pay fees at filing. A redacted public version is required whenever a filing contains non-public information.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 21(a), (b), (h)"},{"t":"Pro hac vice on appeal","d":"An out-of-state lawyer admitted pro hac vice in the trial court files a notice of appearance in the appellate court; no new Bar application is needed.","c":"Utah Sup. Ct. R. Prof. Prac. 14-806(p)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal (civil, as of right)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order (signed and docketed).\nJurisdictional; filed with the trial court clerk. Restarts after a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 73, or 28-day Rule 60(b) motion is decided.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional; filed with the trial court clerk. Restarts after a timely Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 73, or 28-day Rule 60(b) motion is decided."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal, forcible entry or unlawful detainer","d":"10 days from entry of the judgment or order.\nJurisdictional.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(1)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order)","note":"Jurisdictional."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal, order denying anti-SLAPP motion","d":"21 days from entry of the order denying the § 78B-25-103 motion in whole or part.\nAppeal of right for the moving party.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(a)(2); Utah Code § 78B-25-109","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order denying the § 78B-25-103 motion in whole or part)","note":"Appeal of right for the moving party."}},{"t":"Cross-appeal or additional notice","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular period, if later).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Motion to extend notice deadline (excusable neglect)","d":"30 days from expiration of the original appeal period.\nExtension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order, whichever is later.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(e)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the original appeal period)","note":"Extension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 14 days after the order, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion to reinstate appeal time (no notice of judgment)","d":"365 days from entry of judgment.\nStated as “within one year”; notice of appeal then due 30 days after the reinstatement order.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 4(g)","due":{"n":365,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Stated as “within one year”; notice of appeal then due 30 days after the reinstatement order."}},{"t":"Post-judgment motions that toll (Rules 50(b), 52(b), 59)","d":"28 days from entry of judgment.\nNot extendable. Rule 60(b) tolls only if filed within 28 days.","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); 6(b)(2)","due":{"n":28,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Not extendable. Rule 60(b) tolls only if filed within 28 days."}},{"t":"Attorney-fee motion","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nAppeal time runs from disposition of the fee motion or claim (Utah R. App. P. 4(b)(1)(F)).","c":"Utah R. Civ. P. 73(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Appeal time runs from disposition of the fee motion or claim (Utah R. App. P. 4(b)(1)(F))."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","d":"21 days from entry of the trial court’s signed order resolving the motion or issue.\nJurisdictional; 20-page limit; filed in the appellate court.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 5(a)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the trial court’s signed order resolving the motion or issue)","note":"Jurisdictional; 20-page limit; filed in the appellate court."}},{"t":"Docketing statement","d":"21 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nFailure may result in dismissal of a civil appeal.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 9(b)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Failure may result in dismissal of a civil appeal."}},{"t":"Order transcripts (or certify none)","d":"21 days from filing of the docketing statement.\nAppellee cross-designation within 14 days after service.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 11(c)(1)","due":{"n":21,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the docketing statement)","note":"Appellee cross-designation within 14 days after service."}},{"t":"Appellant’s principal brief","d":"40 days from clerk’s Rule 13 briefing notice.\nUp to 60 days total by notice of stipulation (Rule 22(b)).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(1)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (clerk’s Rule 13 briefing notice)","note":"Up to 60 days total by notice of stipulation (Rule 22(b))."}},{"t":"Appellee’s principal brief","d":"30 days from service of appellant’s principal brief.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of appellant’s principal brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"30 days from filing and service of appellee’s principal brief.\nAt least 5 days before argument if argument is set within 35 days.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 26(a)(3)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing and service of appellee’s principal brief)","note":"At least 5 days before argument if argument is set within 35 days."}},{"t":"Paper copies of e-filed briefs","d":"7 days from filing.\n10 copies (Supreme Court) or 6 (Court of Appeals).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 26(d)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing)","note":"10 copies (Supreme Court) or 6 (Court of Appeals)."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"14 days from service of the motion.\nReply within 5 days after the response.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 23(b)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Reply within 5 days after the response."}},{"t":"Retention request after notice of intent to transfer","d":"10 days from issuance of the Supreme Court’s notice of intent to transfer.\nResponse within 5 days.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 42(c)(3)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the Supreme Court’s notice of intent to transfer)","note":"Response within 5 days."}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing","d":"14 days from issuance of the opinion or merits decision.\n15 pages; no response unless requested.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 35(a)(2)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (issuance of the opinion or merits decision)","note":"15 pages; no response unless requested."}},{"t":"Petition for writ of certiorari","d":"30 days from court of Appeals’ final decision (or denial of timely rehearing).\nJurisdictional; one extension of up to 30 days available under Rule 48(e).","c":"Utah R. App. P. 48(a), (c)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (court of Appeals’ final decision)","note":"Jurisdictional; one extension of up to 30 days available under Rule 48(e)."}},{"t":"Response to certiorari petition (only if requested)","d":"30 days from order requesting a response.\nReply within 7 days after the response.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 50(a)(1)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (order requesting a response)","note":"Reply within 7 days after the response."}},{"t":"Bill of costs","d":"14 days from filing of the remittitur with the trial court clerk.\nObjections within 7 days after service.","c":"Utah R. App. P. 34(d)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the remittitur with the trial court clerk)","note":"Objections within 7 days after service."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.utcourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Utah Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"General provisions","s":"Govern all civil actions and statutory proceedings in Utah courts except where other Supreme Court rules or statutes govern, to be construed for the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. The 2026 amendment adds a definition of “district court panel” (a three-judge district court panel convened to hear and decide an action).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"An action starts either by filing a complaint or by serving a summons with a copy of the complaint under Rule 4 (unlike the federal rule). If started by service, the complaint, summons, and proof of service must be filed within 10 days of service or the action is deemed dismissed and the court loses jurisdiction. A dishonored filing-fee payment must be replaced by cash or cashier’s check within 10 days after notice.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"4","t":"Process","s":"The summons is signed and issued by the plaintiff or plaintiff’s attorney, not the clerk. Serve within 120 days after filing the complaint (the federal rule is 90), or the unserved defendant may be dismissed without prejudice. Methods: personal service by a non-party 18 or older; mail or commercial courier with a signed receipt; acceptance of service (including electronic acceptance, which must not be deceptive); Hague or other foreign methods; and court-ordered alternative service on a motion and affidavit showing diligence. A summons for an action begun by service must tell the defendant no answer is needed if the complaint is not filed within 10 days. Utah has no federal-style waiver-of-service cost shifting, only a general duty to avoid service expenses.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every document filed after the complaint must be served on every party, on or before the day it is filed (by the fastest method if a hearing is 7 days away or less). Service is by e-filing if the recipient has an e-filing account, otherwise by email, then mail or hand delivery; mail and electronic service are complete on sending. Serve both the attorney and the party when the attorney entered a Rule 75 limited appearance or more than 90 days have passed after final judgment since anything was served on the attorney. Attorneys with e-filing accounts must e-file; no certificate of service is needed for e-filing service.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Count days by excluding the trigger day and including the last day, rolling past weekends and legal holidays (including Utah’s Pioneer Day and Washington and Lincoln Day). Electronic filing is timely before midnight on the last day. The court may extend time for good cause before expiry or for excusable neglect after, but never the times under Rules 50(b) and (d), 52(b), 59(b), (d), and (e), and 60(c). Add 7 days (the federal rule adds 3) only when service was made exclusively by mail; unrepresented parties without e-filing accounts count from service, not filing; the inmate mailbox rule applies.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; motions, memoranda, hearings, orders","s":"Utah’s detailed motion-practice rule (no federal counterpart of this detail). The motion itself contains the memorandum; opposition is due 14 days after the motion is filed and the reply 7 days after the opposition; evidence objections go in the next memorandum, never in a motion to strike. Nothing is decided until a party files a “Request to Submit for Decision.” Dispositive motions need bold caution language and the bilingual Notice to Responding Party; a hearing must be granted on request for Rule 56 or other dispositive motions unless frivolous or authoritatively decided. Length: 25 pages/9,000 words for Rule 12(b), 12(c), 56, or 65A motions and oppositions (15/5,400 replies); 15/5,400 for other motions and oppositions (10/3,600 replies). A party directed to prepare an order must serve it within 14 days; objections to form are due within 7 days.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"7A","t":"Motion to enforce order and for sanctions","s":"Replaces orders to show cause: file an ex parte verified motion with a proposed “order to attend hearing” (stating contempt penalties of up to a $1,000 fine and 30 days in jail if contempt is sought). Serve the order and papers at least 28 days before the hearing (Rule 4 service if the other side has had no counsel active in the last 120 days). Any opposition is due within 14 days of service of the order; reply within 7 days. Not used for Rule 37(b) sanctions or criminal cases.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"7B","t":"Motion to enforce order and for sanctions in domestic law matters","s":"Domestic-relations version of Rule 7A: serve the order to attend hearing at least 28 days before the hearing; any opposition is due at least 14 days before the hearing and any reply at least 7 days before. Counter motions must be filed with the opposition, and a reply on a counter motion is due at least 3 business days before the hearing by a method ensuring actual receipt.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleadings","s":"Notice-pleading claims with a demand for specified relief. A damages claimant who pleads no amount must plead the Rule 26(c)(3) discovery tier, and pleading tier 1 or 2 waives damages above that tier’s cap unless amended. A pleading seeking relief must carry bold caution language at the top right of page one warning that judgment could be entered; omitting it can support Rule 60(b) relief. Utah’s list of affirmative defenses includes comparative fault.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 9 (particularity for fraud and mistake; general allegations of conditions precedent) and adds Utah provisions: “unknown” defendants in quiet-title actions; general allegations of publication in defamation cases; and allocation of fault to a non-party under Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 5, Part 8, which requires a filed description of the basis and the non-party’s identifying information, no later than 90 days before trial even with leave. Fault cannot be allocated to anyone except by complying with this rule.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings and other papers","s":"Caption must show the court, title, case number, document name, and assigned judge (and commissioner), and any claim for relief must state the Rule 26 discovery tier in the caption and come with the Judicial Council cover sheet. Filer’s name, address, email, phone, and bar number go in the top left corner of page one. Papers are 8½ × 11, 1-inch margins, double-spaced, at least 12-point type. Domestic-relations captions follow set forms (“In the matter of the marriage of …”).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing of pleadings, motions, affidavits, and other papers; representations to court; sanctions","s":"Tracks federal Rule 11, including the 21-day safe harbor before a sanctions motion may be filed. An unsworn declaration under the Uniform Unsworn Declarations Act (Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 18a) may replace an affidavit or notarized signature.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections","s":"Answer within 21 days after service in Utah, or 30 days if served outside Utah (no federal-style 60-day waiver period); crossclaim and counterclaim answers are due in 21 days. After a Rule 12 motion is denied or deferred, answer within 14 days. Defenses (1)-(7) mirror federal Rule 12(b), with “failure to join an indispensable party” as (7); consolidation and waiver rules track federal law. Utah-only: a defendant may move to require an out-of-state or foreign-corporation plaintiff to post a $300 cost undertaking, and the action is dismissed on motion if it is not filed within 30 days of service of the order.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and crossclaim","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 13: compulsory counterclaims from the same transaction or occurrence, permissive counterclaims, after-acquired counterclaims by supplemental pleading, and crossclaims against coparties.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may serve a third-party complaint without leave if it is filed within 14 days after serving the original answer; later requires leave on motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead likewise.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"Amend once as of course within 21 days after serving the pleading, or 21 days after service of a responsive pleading or a Rule 12(b), (e), or (f) motion, whichever is earlier; otherwise by leave (attach the proposed amended pleading) or written consent. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left on the original or 14 days after service, whichever is later. Relation back tracks federal Rule 15(c), keyed to the Rule 4(b) 120-day service period.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences","s":"The court may hold scheduling and management conferences and set deadlines for joinder, amendment, motions, and discovery. Unless an order sets the trial date, the plaintiff must certify at the close of discovery that discovery and any required ADR are complete and the case is ready for trial (a certificate of readiness). Failure to attend, prepare, or participate in good faith allows Rule 37(b) sanctions. There is no federal-style mandatory scheduling order deadline.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant","s":"Real party in interest, with time to ratify or substitute before dismissal. Minors and incompetent persons appear by a general guardian or a guardian ad litem; a minor defendant 14 or older may apply for a guardian ad litem within 21 days after service, and a guardian ad litem for a nonresident minor defendant has 21 days after appointment to plead. Associations may sue or be sued in a common name; nonresidents doing business in Utah may be sued in their business name.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 18: unlimited joinder of claims against an opposing party, and a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a voidable transaction without first obtaining judgment.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Older federal Rule 19 language: required parties must be joined if feasible; if not, the court decides in equity and good conscience whether to proceed, treating the absent person as indispensable if the action is dismissed. A claimant must plead the names of required persons not joined and why.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 20: same transaction or series plus a common question; the court may order separate trials to prevent prejudice.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage on just terms.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Rule interpleader when the plaintiff faces double or multiple liability, available by crossclaim or counterclaim; claims need not share a common origin.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Utah’s text follows the pre-2003 federal Rule 23: the (a) prerequisites, (b)(1)-(3) categories, certification “as soon as practicable,” best-practicable notice with opt-out in (b)(3) classes, and court approval with notice for any dismissal or compromise. The collected text has no counterpart to federal Rule 23(e)-(h) settlement-review standards, 23(f) interlocutory appeal of certification, or class-counsel appointment provisions.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"23A","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"Verified complaint alleging contemporaneous ownership, non-collusion, and with particularity the demand made or why none was made. Dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice to shareholders or members.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention track the federal rule. A party challenging the constitutionality of a Utah statute where the Attorney General has not appeared must serve a “Notice of Constitutional Challenge Under URCP 24(d)” on the Attorney General (by email to notices@agutah.gov) on or before filing the challenge, and file proof of service; similar notice goes to a governmental entity whose ordinance or rule is challenged. The responding entity has 14 days after the parties’ briefing deadline to file a notice of intent to respond (up to 7 more days on request) and 14 days after that to respond. ICWA tribes may participate without formally intervening.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"Move to substitute within 90 days after a death is suggested on the record, or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. Covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and public officers (substitution of a successor officer within 6 months after the successor takes office on a showing of substantial need).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing disclosure and discovery","s":"Utah’s proportionality and tiered-discovery system, the biggest difference from federal practice. Initial disclosures (witnesses with summaries of expected testimony for those a party may call, copies of documents it may offer, damages computation with supporting documents, insurance, and documents referred to in pleadings) are due from a plaintiff 14 days after the first answer and from a defendant 42 days after its first answer; no discovery may be sought until a party’s own disclosures are served. Scope is relevant, non-privileged matter that is proportional, and the party seeking discovery always bears the burden of showing proportionality and relevance. Standard fact discovery per side by tier (damages in the original pleadings): Tier 1 ($50,000 or less) 3 deposition hours, 0 interrogatories, 5 document requests, 5 admission requests, 120 days; Tier 2 (more than $50,000 and less than $300,000, or non-monetary relief) 15 hours, 10/10/10, 180 days; Tier 3 ($300,000 or more) 30 hours, 20/20/20, 210 days; Tier 4 (domestic relations) 4 hours, 10/10/10, 90 days, with days counted from when the first defendant’s first disclosure is due. Going beyond requires, before standard discovery closes and after its limits are reached, a stipulated statement (with attorney-client consultation certifications) or a Rule 37(a) request. Experts: the party with the burden discloses within 14 days after fact discovery closes; the opponent then has 14 days to elect a deposition (4-hour cap, deposing party pays) or a signed report (offering party pays), which must occur or be served within 42 days; the other side’s and rebuttal disclosures follow in 14-day steps, and without an election there is no further expert discovery. Pretrial disclosures are due 28 days before trial, counter-designations and objections 14 days before trial (objections other than under Utah R. Evid. 402 and 403 are waived). Undisclosed witnesses and documents are excluded unless the failure is harmless or for good cause. Discovery papers are not filed; only a certificate of service is.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"26.1","t":"Disclosure and discovery in domestic relations actions","s":"In divorce, separation, parentage, custody, support, and modification cases, disclosures are due 14 days after the first answer, including a court-form Financial Declaration with attachments: 2 years of tax returns, 12 months of income proof and loan applications, real-estate valuations, and 3 months of statements for every financial account. Where assets are not at issue, a short list suffices (3 paystubs, last tax return, 6 months of bank and P&L statements if self-employed). Incomplete disclosure can lead to Rule 37 sanctions, including awarding undisclosed assets to the other party.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"26.2","t":"Disclosures in personal injury actions","s":"Adds to Rule 26(a) in physical-injury cases: the plaintiff lists treating providers for the injury and all providers for the prior 5 years, gives SSN or Medicare number, benefits and 5 years of employers if wage loss is claimed, bills, official reports, and witness statements. The defendant discloses insurance limits, excess coverage, deductibles, and reservations of rights (declarations page suffices unless the full policy is requested), reports, statements, and Rule 9(l) fault-allocation information.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"26.3","t":"Disclosure in unlawful detainer actions","s":"In evictions the plaintiff serves the rental agreement, eviction notice, itemized amounts, and factual basis with the summons and complaint. Disclosures for an evidentiary hearing under Utah Code § 78B-6-810 go with the request or at least 2 days before the hearing; pretrial disclosures are due 14 days before trial and objections 7 days before.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"26.4","t":"Provisions governing disclosure and discovery in contested proceedings under Title 75 of the Utah Code","s":"In contested probate, guardianship, and trust matters, the petitioner is presumed the plaintiff and objectors defendants. An oral objection at a hearing must be put in writing and filed within 7 days. Nomination documents (wills, trusts, powers of attorney) and, in guardianships, less-restrictive alternatives must be served within 14 days after a written objection; originals whose validity is disputed must be made available within 14 days of referral to mediation.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"Verified petition to perpetuate testimony in the county where an expected adverse party resides, with notice served at least 21 days before the hearing in the Rule 4(d) manner. The trial court may also allow depositions pending appeal.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Before an officer authorized to administer oaths, a court appointee, or in a foreign country on notice, by commission, or by letter rogatory. No deposition before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or anyone financially interested.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding disclosure and discovery procedure","s":"Amended effective November 1, 2026: parties may expand discovery by filing, before standard discovery closes and after reaching its limits, a stipulated statement that the extraordinary discovery is necessary and proportional and that each party has reviewed and approved a discovery budget. Stipulations only extending time need no proportionality or budget statement and need court approval only if they would interfere with a discovery deadline, hearing, or trial date.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral questions","s":"A witness may be deposed only once in standard discovery, and an expert who gave a Rule 26(a)(4)(B) report may not be deposed. During standard discovery, questioning of a nonparty is capped at 4 hours and of a party at 7 hours, all within the tier’s total deposition hours. An organization served with a Rule 30(b)(6)-style notice may object in writing within 14 days, and the parties must confer before the deposition or the objections are waived. Remote depositions are allowed and are taken where the witness is. Objections must be concise and non-suggestive; instructions not to answer only for privilege, a court limit, or a protective-order motion. The witness has 28 days after notice that the transcript is available to submit changes. Out-of-state actions may depose Utah witnesses as if pending in Utah, filing notice with the clerk of the witness’s county.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Cross questions within 14 days after service of the questions, redirect within 7 days, re-cross within 7 days. In standard discovery, written-question depositions are capped at 15 questions (including discrete subparts) per side cumulatively.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 32: impeachment use, adverse use of a party or organizational designee’s deposition, and use of an unavailable witness’s deposition (dead, more than 100 miles away, ill, or not subpoenable, or exceptional circumstances). Objections to the form of written questions are waived unless served within the time for the next round and within 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Allowed only within Rule 26(c)(5) tier limits (none in Tier 1; 10 in Tiers 2 and 4; 20 in Tier 3, including discrete subparts). Answers or objections within 28 days (the federal rule gives 30), restating each interrogatory; unstated objections are waived. Business-records option as in federal Rule 33(d).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things and entry upon land for inspection and other purposes","s":"Respond in writing within 28 days, restating each request; objections must be specific and say whether anything is withheld, and stating the search terms used counts as notice that items outside them may be withheld. Tier limits on the number of requests apply. ESI is produced in the form requested or as ordinarily maintained or reasonably usable, and only in one form.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examination of persons","s":"Court-ordered on motion for good cause; the examinee may audio- or video-record the exam unless the requesting party shows it would unduly interfere (unlike federal practice). The requesting party must disclose the examiner’s detailed report within the shorter of 60 days after the exam or 7 days before fact discovery closes. Noncompliance allows Rule 37(b) sanctions but not contempt.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"36","t":"Request for admission","s":"Requests must carry bold caution language on page one warning that matters are admitted if not answered within 28 days; omitting it may support Rule 60(b) relief. Matters are admitted unless a written response is served within 28 days (restating each request). Tier limits apply (5 in Tier 1; 10 in Tiers 2 and 4; 20 in Tier 3). Admissions bind only in the pending action and may be withdrawn if it promotes the merits without prejudice.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"37","t":"Statement of discovery issues; Sanctions; Failure to admit, to attend deposition or to preserve evidence","s":"Discovery disputes (compel, protective orders, extraordinary discovery, subpoenas) go by a “statement of discovery issues” of no more than 4 pages or 1,500 words, with a meet-and-confer certification (in person or by phone), a proportionality statement, and, for extraordinary discovery, certification of a reviewed and approved discovery budget (budget certification added November 1, 2026). Objections are due within 7 days (4 pages); each side files a proposed order, and the filer must request submission. The statement may seek fees but not sanctions and does not toll discovery deadlines. Sanctions for violating orders, including adverse-inference instructions, require a separate motion; failure to admit shifts proof costs; failure to appear at a deposition is not excused by objections unless a statement of discovery issues was filed. No sanctions, absent exceptional circumstances, for ESI lost through routine good-faith system operation.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"Demand a jury in writing and pay the statutory jury fee no later than 14 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue; others may demand on other issues within 14 days of service of the demand. Failure to pay the fee or to serve and file the demand waives the jury (fee payment is a Utah-specific condition).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Demanded issues are tried to a jury unless the parties stipulate otherwise, no jury right exists, or a party fails to appear at trial. The court may allow a late jury on motion and may use an advisory jury.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"40","t":"Scheduling and postponing a trial","s":"The court need not set trial until the case is certified ready under Rule 16; trial may be postponed for good cause on just terms, and witness testimony may be preserved at a hearing for use like a deposition.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"Voluntary dismissal by notice before an answer or summary judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all appearing parties; a second notice dismissal of the same claim operates as an adjudication on the merits. Involuntary dismissal for failure to prosecute or comply is on the merits unless the order says otherwise. A counterclaim, crossclaim, or third-party claimant may voluntarily dismiss only before a responsive pleading is served (or before evidence at trial). A refiling plaintiff may be ordered to pay costs of the earlier action.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials; venue transfer","s":"Consolidation may reach actions in other judicial districts; the motion is filed in and heard by the judge with the first-filed action, with notice filed in each case. In medical malpractice cases, past medical expenses are kept from the factfinder until liability and noneconomic damages are resolved. Improper venue must be transferred on timely motion, with substantial deference to a plaintiff’s proper venue choice. A party may request transfer to the Business and Chancery Court; if requested within 21 days after the party’s appearance, transfer is mandatory unless it would prejudice the interests of justice. New in 2026: a party may file a notice to convene a three-judge district court panel under Utah Code § 78A-5-102.7 within 45 days after the action is commenced or an amended complaint is filed (for cases pending on February 13, 2026, within 45 days of that date), after which the assigned judge must transfer the case and take no further action.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"43","t":"Evidence","s":"Testimony is taken in open court, but in civil cases the court may allow remote testimony for good cause with required safeguards (notice, verbatim record, confidential attorney-client channel, document sharing); a special remote oath bars communicating with anyone during testimony. Motions on facts outside the record may be heard on affidavits, declarations, testimony, or depositions.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Official records are proved by official publication or an attested copy with a custodian certificate, including foreign-record certifications by U.S. consular officers. Certified copies of Utah or U.S. records may be read in evidence; foreign and sister-state law may be proved by official publications, and the court determines it.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Utah-admitted attorneys may issue subpoenas as officers of the court; the clerk issues blank signed subpoenas to others. Serve as in Rule 4(d) by a non-party adult, tendering one day’s fees and mileage for appearances. A document or inspection subpoena must first be served on every party by a method giving actual notice, must allow at least 14 days to comply, and may require a Utah resident to appear for deposition or production only in the county where the person resides, works, or does business in person (a non-resident served in Utah, only in the county of service). Written objections before the compliance date stop compliance on the objected topics until the issuing party obtains an order under Rule 37(a). Responders serve a declaration of completeness and cost, and the issuing party pays reasonable production costs.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; a party preserves an issue by stating the desired action or objection and grounds when the ruling is made.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"47","t":"Jurors","s":"The court or counsel may conduct voir dire. Each side gets 3 peremptory challenges (federal practice also uses 3), plus 1 more if one or two alternates are called; multiple parties on a side are one party unless their interests substantially conflict. Lists statutory and impartiality grounds for cause; the judge picks strike-and-replace or struck method. Jurors may submit written questions if the judge allows, may take notes into deliberations, and a verdict requires three-fourths of the jurors unless the parties agree otherwise (non-unanimous civil verdicts, unlike federal court).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"48","t":"Juries of less than eight - Majority verdict","s":"The parties may stipulate to a jury of fewer than eight or to a verdict by a stated majority.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"Mirrors federal Rule 49: special verdicts (omitted issues are waived unless submission is demanded before the jury retires) and general verdicts with interrogatories, with rules for inconsistent answers.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law in a jury trial; related motion for a new trial; conditional ruling","s":"JMOL may be sought any time before submission. A renewed motion is due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment (or after the jury is discharged on an undecided issue), optionally joined with a new-trial request; a losing party’s new-trial motion after JMOL is due 28 days after that judgment. These times cannot be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury; objections","s":"Requested instructions are filed at the final pretrial conference or when the court directs. Object to written instructions before they are given (oral ones before the jury retires), stating the matter and grounds; unpreserved errors are reviewable only to avoid manifest injustice. Closing arguments come after final instructions, and the court may not comment on the evidence.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings and conclusions by the court; amended findings; waiver of findings and conclusions; correction of the record; judgment on partial findings","s":"Bench trials require separate findings and conclusions, reviewed for clear error; findings are not required on Rule 12(b), 50, 56, or 59 motions, but a brief statement of grounds is required when a motion rests on more than one ground. Motion to amend findings within 28 days after judgment (not extendable). Findings may be waived except in divorce actions. A motion to correct the transcript is due within 14 days after it is filed.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Reference to a master is the exception; the first meeting is set within 21 days after the order of reference. In non-jury cases the court accepts the master’s findings unless clearly erroneous, and objections to the report are due within 14 days after notice of its filing. Parties may object to a master’s appointment on juror-challenge grounds.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"53A","t":"Special masters for parenting disputes in domestic relations actions","s":"Only on the parties’ stipulation and after a parenting plan or order is entered, the court may appoint a special master to issue directives on parenting disputes, effective as orders. A party may move to modify or set aside a directive within 14 days; the court reviews de novo. Special masters cannot find contempt or issue sanctions unless authorized, and neither party may end the appointment unilaterally.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Rule 54(b) certification requires an express finding of no just reason for delay; otherwise partial rulings remain revisable. File and serve a verified memorandum of costs within 14 days after entry of judgment; objections are due 7 days after service. When costs or fees are awarded after judgment, the prevailing party files an amended judgment, which is entered unless someone objects within 7 days.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"55","t":"Default","s":"The clerk enters default for failure to plead or defend. The clerk may enter a sum-certain default judgment only if the defendant failed to appear, is not a minor or incompetent, was personally served under Rule 4(d)(1), and the amount is verified by verified complaint, affidavit, or unsworn declaration; otherwise apply to the court. Defaults are set aside for good cause, default judgments under Rule 60(b); no default judgment against the State without satisfactory evidence.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"The motion must contain a statement of material facts in separately numbered paragraphs with record citations; the opposition must restate verbatim each disputed fact and explain the dispute with citations, and undisputed facts are deemed admitted. A claimant may move after 21 days from commencement (or after the adverse party moves); a defending party any time; absent an order, no later than 28 days after the close of all discovery. Rule 56(d) relief, failure-to-address consequences, and bad-faith affidavit sanctions track the federal rule. Rule 7 applies (25 pages/9,000 words, and a hearing on request).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Declaratory actions under Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 6, Part 4 follow these rules; jury trial may be demanded under Rules 38-39; another adequate remedy does not bar relief, and the court may expedite.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"58A","t":"Entry of judgment; abstract of judgment","s":"Every judgment needs a separate document (not required for orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 59, 60, or 73 motions). The prevailing party serves a proposed judgment within 14 days after verdict or decision; objections to form are due within 7 days. A judgment requiring a separate document is entered when signed and docketed or 150 days after the decision is docketed, whichever is earlier (federal law also uses 150 days). A pending fee claim does not affect finality, but appeal time runs from its disposition under Appellate Rule 4 (except as Rule 73(g) provides). The preparing party must serve the signed judgment promptly.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"58B","t":"Satisfaction of judgment","s":"The judgment creditor must file an acknowledgment of satisfaction within 28 days after full satisfaction (in both district and juvenile court for juvenile restitution judgments); the court may also declare satisfaction on motion.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"58C","t":"Motion to renew judgment","s":"Renew a judgment by Rule 7 motion in the original action before the limitations period on the judgment runs, with an affidavit accounting for payments and confirming notice to the debtor’s last known address. The renewed judgment runs from the order date or the original expiration date, whichever is first.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"59","t":"New trial; altering or amending a judgment","s":"Lists 7 grounds, including insufficiency of the evidence and error of law. Motions for new trial and to alter or amend are due no later than 28 days after entry of judgment, with supporting affidavits for grounds (a)(1)-(4); the court may order a new trial on its own within 28 days. These times cannot be extended.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical errors may be fixed any time (with appellate leave once an appeal is pending). Grounds track federal Rule 60(b), but motions for mistake, excusable neglect, newly discovered evidence, or fraud must be filed within 90 days after entry of the judgment or order (the federal limit is one year); other grounds within a reasonable time. The motion does not suspend finality.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"No execution for 28 days after entry of a judgment or a Rule 7(j)(8) order to pay money. A stay is available by bond or other security at any time after judgment; the presumptive amount for compensatory damages is the damages plus costs and fees plus 3 years of interest, capped at $25 million in class or aggregate multi-plaintiff actions, with no bond for punitive damages. Personal sureties must be Utah residents with net worth at least twice the bond. Governmental appellants need no bond. In domestic cases custody, parent-time, current child support, and current alimony cannot be stayed on appeal (with narrow exceptions).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"63","t":"Disability or disqualification of a judge","s":"A motion to disqualify for bias, prejudice, or conflict needs a good-faith certificate, a supporting affidavit or unsworn declaration, and a request to submit, and must be filed no later than 21 days after the latest of assignment, the party’s appearance, or when the party knew or should have known the grounds (as soon as practicable if that falls within 21 days of a hearing). Only one motion per party absent newly discovered grounds. The challenged judge must, without a response, either grant it or certify it to a reviewing judge and take no further action. Amended effective February 13, 2026 to cover judges on three-judge district court panels (the Judicial Council’s presiding officer reviews; a panel as a whole cannot be disqualified). The rule text notes the February 13, 2026 date depends on a constitutional two-thirds legislative vote.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"63A","t":"Change of judge as a matter of right","s":"New February 13, 2026: in a civil action in a county with seven or more district court judges, each side (only two sides, regardless of adverse interests) gets one peremptory change of judge by filing a notice with the clerk, giving no reason but attesting it is not for delay or because of race, gender, or religious affiliation. Deadlines: plaintiff side within 7 days after a judge is first assigned; defendant side within 7 days after service of the complaint or at its first filing, whichever comes first; for a district court panel, within 7 days after notice of the assigned judges. Not available in Rule 65C cases, juvenile court, the Business and Chancery Court, water or tax cases, on remand, or on most modification petitions before the same judge. Separately, all parties may unanimously change the judge once without cause within 90 days after commencement or before the trial-setting notice, whichever is first, without telling the court who sought consent. The rule text says the February 13, 2026 effective date depends on a constitutional two-thirds legislative vote; confirm status.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64","t":"Writs in general","s":"Common definitions and procedures for writs of replevin, attachment, garnishment, and execution. The court sets security (none for governmental entities); objections to security are due within 5 days after service of the writ. The clerk issues writs, the officer returns the writ within 14 days after service, and only one earnings garnishment may be in effect at a time. Third-party claimants have the defendant’s rights and, if joined, answer within 14 days; the defendant may move to discharge the writ by posting security (plaintiff objects within 7 days) or as wrongfully obtained.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64A","t":"Prejudgment writs in general","s":"Prejudgment replevin, attachment, or garnishment only by court order on motion, security, and a non-conclusory affidavit showing non-exempt, non-earnings property, no intent to defraud, a substantial likelihood of success, and at least one listed ground (such as concealment, flight, fraud, or decline in value). The defendant may reply at least 24 hours before the hearing. An ex parte writ requires irreparable-injury facts and expires 14 days after issuance unless extended.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64B","t":"Writ of replevin","s":"Compels delivery of specific personal property; requires that the plaintiff is entitled to possession and the defendant wrongfully detains it, plus the Rule 64A grounds before judgment.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64C","t":"Writ of attachment","s":"Seizes property held by the defendant; before judgment also requires that the defendant is indebted to the plaintiff, the action is on a contract or against a nonresident or unqualified foreign corporation (or a statute authorizes it), and the claim is not secured by a Utah lien.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64D","t":"Writ of garnishment","s":"Reaches the defendant’s property held by third parties, before or after judgment. Earnings garnishment is capped at the lesser of 25% of disposable earnings (50% for child-support judgments) or the excess over 30 times the federal minimum hourly wage per week. The garnishee answers interrogatories and serves the defendant within 7 business days; replies and hearing requests are due within 14 days after service of the answers, with a hearing within 14 days; the garnishee holds property 21 days after its service before delivering. A continuing garnishment lasts up to one year (or 120 days after a later writ is served), with answers within 7 days after each pay period.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"64E","t":"Writ of execution","s":"After final judgment, on an application stating the amount due and the property. The defendant may reply and request a hearing within 14 days after service, and the hearing must be held within 14 days; no sale until the issues are decided. Mortgage foreclosure is governed by Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 6, Part 9.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"65A","t":"Injunctions","s":"Preliminary injunctions require notice. An ex parte TRO requires specific facts of immediate irreparable injury and a written certification of notice efforts, and expires within no more than 14 days unless extended for a like period; the restrained party may move to dissolve on 48 hours’ notice. Grounds: substantial likelihood of success (Utah’s rule no longer allows “serious issues” as a substitute), irreparable harm, balance of harms, and public interest. Security is required unless excused (none from governmental entities). For a constitutional challenge to a state law that takes effect more than 60 days after the session ends, a TRO motion must be filed at least 45 days before the effective date or not until the law has been in effect 90 days, and the State’s response to a TRO or preliminary-injunction motion against a not-yet-effective law is due within 28 days.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"65B","t":"Extraordinary relief","s":"Replaces the common-law writs (habeas corpus outside Rule 65C, quo warranto, mandamus, certiorari, prohibition) with a single petition when no plain, speedy, and adequate remedy exists. Covers wrongful restraint of liberty, wrongful use of public or corporate authority (generally brought by the Attorney General; a private petitioner must post an undertaking), and wrongful use of judicial authority, failure to perform a duty, or Board of Pardons and Parole actions. Frivolous claims are summarily dismissed; review of judicial proceedings is limited to whether the respondent regularly pursued its authority.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"65C","t":"Post-conviction relief","s":"Governs petitions under the Post-Conviction Remedies Act, Utah Code Title 78B, Chapter 9, filed in the county of conviction and assigned to the sentencing judge. The court must decide procedural bars under § 78B-9-106 before commenting on the merits; facially frivolous or previously adjudicated claims are summarily dismissed (not an initial petition in a death case), and deficient claims get 21 days to amend (one more 21-day period for good cause). The respondent answers within 30 days after service, and the petitioner may respond to dispositive motions within 30 days; discovery only for good cause. An order vacating a felony conviction is stayed 7 days for the State’s election.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers","s":"Grounds for appointing a receiver include property at risk, enforcing a judgment, an unsatisfied execution, and corporate insolvency or dissolution. A party, attorney, or non-disinterested person may serve only with all interested parties’ written consent. Taxes must be paid before personal property is sold, with receipts filed within 14 days after payment; the appointment order must be recorded before the receiver takes real property.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"The court may order money or things held for or due to another party deposited in court or delivered, with deposits handled under Utah Code § 78B-5-804.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"68","t":"Settlement offers","s":"Unlike federal Rule 68, either party may make an offer. The offer must be in writing, cite the rule, be made more than 14 days before trial, stay open at least 14 days, and be served under Rule 5. If the offeree’s “adjusted award” (verdict plus pre-offer costs, interest, and fees unless excluded) is not more favorable, the offeror owes no post-offer costs, prejudgment interest, or fees, and the offeree pays the offeror’s post-offer costs; the court may suspend the rule to prevent manifest injustice.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"69A","t":"Seizure of property","s":"The officer seizes the property the debtor designates, otherwise personal property before real property. Real property is seized by recording the writ with the county recorder and leaving it with an occupant or posting it; farm products, securities, and bulky property have special methods.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"69B","t":"Sale of property; delivery of property","s":"Execution sales are auctions Monday through Saturday (not legal holidays) between 9:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m. Personal property: post notice at least 7 days and publish once at least a day before the sale; real property: post at least 21 days and publish weekly for 3 successive weeks, with the sale at the county’s district courthouse. Postponements longer than 72 hours require new notice. A bid is an irrevocable offer.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"69C","t":"Redemption of real property after sale","s":"Real property sold on execution may be redeemed within 180 days after the sale by the debtor or a junior lienholder, at the sale price plus 6% (later redemptions plus 3%), plus recorded taxes and other outlays. A price dispute requires paying the undisputed amount into court and petitioning within 21 days; an accounting request extends redemption to 7 days after the accounting.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform a judgment-ordered act, the court may appoint someone to do it at the party’s cost, issue attachment or sequestration, hold the party in contempt, or vest title directly by judgment.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"Non-parties may enforce orders in their favor, and be subject to enforcement, like parties.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"72","t":"Property bonds","s":"A real-property bond must be signed and notarized by all record owners, with legal description, title and tax documents, a title or foreclosure report, and statements from each lienholder; it is effective only when recorded with the county recorder.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"73","t":"Attorney fees","s":"Claim fees by motion no later than 14 days after judgment is entered, specifying the basis, amount, and reasonableness, with an affidavit itemizing time by person, position, and rate (and disclosure and a Rule 5.4 statement for fees for assignees or debt collectors). If liability is decided, a proposed order on an affidavit is entered unless someone objects within 7 days. Scheduled fees without affidavit: up to $475 on an uncontested judgment and $1,020 after a contested one when the complaint pleaded the fee basis; $105 for a writ application or listed motion and $35 for repeat garnishments of the same garnishee or motions within 6 months.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"74","t":"Withdrawal of counsel","s":"An attorney may withdraw by notice only if no motion is pending, no hearing or trial is set, and no court-ordered obligation is outstanding; otherwise by motion and order. The order tells the client to appoint counsel or appear personally within 21 days, during which proceedings are stayed, and that entities must appear through a Utah-admitted attorney. When counsel otherwise leaves, the opposing party must serve a notice to appear or appoint counsel, which stays proceedings 21 days. Substitution of counsel needs no court approval if new counsel certifies compliance with the existing schedule. Amended November 1, 2026.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"75","t":"Limited appearance","s":"An attorney may appear for limited purposes (a filing, a motion, a discovery event, a hearing, or another purpose with leave) by filing a Notice of Limited Appearance signed by attorney and client describing the scope; the party remains responsible for everything else. Both the attorney and party must be served on matters within the scope (Rule 5).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"76","t":"Notice of contact information change","s":"Attorneys and self-represented parties must promptly notify the court and other parties in writing of any change in address, email, or phone, unless disclosure is barred by order or law.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"77","t":"District courts and clerks","s":"District courts are always open for filing; merits trials are in open court, but other proceedings may be held in chambers anywhere in Utah, except non-ex parte hearings outside the county of the case require all affected parties’ consent. Clerks may grant process, defaults, and default judgments of course.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"81","t":"Applicability of rules in general","s":"The rules apply to special statutory proceedings unless clearly inapplicable; not to uncontested probate and guardianship (they apply once issues are joined) or to small claims unless incorporated. In the Business and Chancery Court they apply except where the Business and Chancery rules have a same-numbered rule or exclude one by number. They govern review of agency action unless the statute conflicts, and gaps in criminal proceedings.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction and venue unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit jurisdiction or venue.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"83","t":"Vexatious litigants","s":"A person (including an attorney acting pro se) may be declared vexatious for: at least 5 claims finally lost in the past 7 years without 2 wins (small claims excluded); 2 or more attempts to relitigate a decided claim or issue; 3 or more unmeritorious, scandalous, disproportionate, or harassing filings or tactics in an action; or invoking non-recognized “courts.” On clear and convincing findings, the court may require security, counsel, or prefiling permission (never for a notice of appeal). Limitations periods are tolled until 7 days after notice of a decision on a filing application; a pending-action prefiling order may be challenged after 1 year, a future-claims order after 5 years.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"Cited as the Utah Rules of Civil Procedure (U.R.C.P.).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"86","t":"Licensed paralegal practitioners","s":"Licensed paralegal practitioners (LPPs) are treated as attorneys within their limited license and the practice areas in Rule 14-802; they may sign and file only on court-approved forms or consistent with Judicial Council forms, and may serve disclosures. They recover fees only with a supporting affidavit (no Rule 73(f) schedule). An LPP who only prepares a paper need not appear. Amended November 1, 2026.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"87","t":"In-person, remote, and hybrid hearings; request for different format","s":"The court sets each hearing as in-person, remote, or hybrid after weighing listed factors. A participant (through counsel if represented) may request a different format by motion, email, or letter (with a prescribed subject line, copied to all parties) at least 7 days before the hearing, and the court must accommodate a timely request unless it finds good cause on the record.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"100","t":"Coordination of cases pending in district court and juvenile court","s":"In custody, support, or parent-time cases, parties must notify the court of related custody, support, criminal, delinquency, or protective-order cases (caption, number, judge) with the initial pleading or as soon as practicable; judges and commissioners must confer about consolidation or coordination.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"100A","t":"Case Management of Domestic Relations Actions","s":"After an answer, domestic cases (other than Office of Recovery Services cases) get a case management conference and are placed in Track 1 (standard, certified for trial after mediation), Track 2 (complex discovery, such as business valuation, with a set discovery schedule), or Track 3 (significant custody disputes, addressing custody evaluations and private guardians ad litem).","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"101","t":"Motion practice before court commissioners","s":"Motions heard by court commissioners (mainly domestic cases) must carry bold caution language and the bilingual notice, with evidence by affidavit or declaration. Serve the motion and notice of hearing at least 28 days before the hearing (Rule 4 service if the other party has filed or served nothing in 120 days); response at least 14 days before; reply at least 7 days before; replies on countermotions at least 3 business days before by hand or electronic delivery. Each party has a 25-page total limit per hearing, with listed financial and legal documents excluded; collections of documents over 10 pages must be summarized. Temporary-order motions on support or finances need verified financial declarations (and a child support worksheet). Listed motions, such as alternative service and motions in limine, go to the judge.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"102","t":"Motion and order for payment of costs and fees","s":"In actions under Utah Code § 81-1-203(1), either party may move, with an affidavit, for the other to pay costs, attorney fees, and witness and expert fees needed to prosecute or defend; the court must find need, ability to pay, necessity, and reasonableness. The award is due in a lump sum within 30 days unless ordered otherwise.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"103","t":"Child protective orders","s":"When juvenile court transfers a child protective order petition to district court, a hearing must be held within 21 days of any ex parte order. A child may be compelled to testify only for extenuating circumstances, with procedures for recorded statements, recorded testimony, and testimony outside the courtroom. Amended November 1, 2026.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"104","t":"Divorce decree upon affidavit","s":"A divorce decree may be entered without a hearing on an affidavit with sufficient evidence when the other party defaults, waives notice, or stipulates.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"105","t":"Shortening 30 day waiting period in divorce actions","s":"A request to enter a divorce decree less than 30 days after filing requires an affidavit stating the filing date and the extraordinary circumstances.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"106","t":"Modification of final domestic relations order","s":"Modification starts with a petition served with a summons under Rule 4 (except as Utah Code § 81-9-209 provides); answer within Rule 12 time. The existing order stays in effect, a modification may be retroactive to service of the petition, and temporary changes to custody, parent-time, and related support are allowed in limited situations.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"107","t":"Decree of adoption; Petition to open adoption records","s":"A petition to open adoption records must state good cause and why the Office of Vital Records histories or voluntary registry is insufficient; the court balances disclosure against confidentiality, limits inspection to what serves the purpose, and the clerk reseals the records.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"108","t":"Objection to court commissioner’s recommendation","s":"A commissioner’s recommendation is the court’s order until modified. Object in writing within 14 days after the recommendation is made in open court (or after service of the minute entry if taken under advisement), with Rule 7 briefing. New evidence is barred unless circumstances have substantially changed; in domestic matters a party may request an evidentiary hearing on custody facts. The judge makes independent findings.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"109","t":"Injunction in certain domestic relations cases","s":"On filing a divorce, custody, support, parentage, or similar petition, the court enters a standard mutual injunction: no transfer of property outside the ordinary course, harassment, use of the other’s identity for credit, cancellation of insurance, or non-routine travel with a child without an itinerary, and no disparaging the other parent in the child’s presence. It binds the petitioner on filing and the respondent on receipt. Before a responsive pleading, a motion to modify requires at least 48 hours’ notice.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"},{"n":"110","t":"Judicial interview of a minor child","s":"New November 1, 2026: a judge or commissioner may interview a child only if extenuating circumstances require it, no other reasonable method exists, and it is in the child’s best interest, after Judicial Council training. Parties may propose questions; the interview is recorded; parties and their attorneys are excluded except in extraordinary circumstances; disputed potentially dispositive factual allegations must be disclosed to the parties for response.","u":"https://legacy.utcourts.gov/rules/viewall.php?type=urcp"}]});
